$DIR/main.p:36:14: data holds a view of f (ReadAll(&f), line 34), which was passed to f.Close(), which may free what it looks at or move it out (f.raw = ..., line 20) at line 35, so data can no longer be used here
    data looks at memory that f owns, without owning it: the view was made by
    ReadAll(&f) at line 34. A view of an owner held in a local variable may be
    used only while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. A call
    that may free memory the view looks at, or move an owner out of it, through
    a pointer to the variable or to memory it owns ends the view as well. At
    line 35 f was passed to f.Close(), which may free what it looks at or move
    it out (f.raw = ..., line 20), so the view may look at memory that was
    released, or at a value that now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use data before line 35, or make that call only after the last use of data
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)

